    "ceiling_height_rule": {
      "group": "interior",
      "binding": "specified",
      "status": "drafted",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "judgment": true,
      "parameters": {
        "storey_height_ratio": {
          "set": [
            10,
            9,
            7.5
          ],
          "unit": "ratio",
          "kind": "measured",
          "source": "italian-villa-vernacular.json typical_ratios ('Storey heights diminishing upward roughly 10:9:7.5')"
        }
      },
      "rule": "Ceiling height is not derived from a room's own plan proportion by a harmonic mean. It is set by storey, diminishing upward roughly 10:9:7.5 from the ground floor to the top storey, consistent with the style's own governing_logic ('proportion is set by the storey height and the opening, not by a module').",
      "note": "OVERRIDES a cascade fill from renaissance-classical (a harmonic-mean-of-plan-proportion rule, reached via the italian-renaissance hybridization edge) that contradicts this node's own bound room-role proportion pack: this style's own proportion_packs binds `room-vernacular` (precedence 2, span/storey-driven) as its room pack and explicitly rejects the module-and-order system room-harmonic assumes, and its own governing_logic states plainly there is 'no module' governing rooms. Confirmed with `resolve_kit.py italian-villa-vernacular --slot ceiling_height_rule --verbose`: the PACK RESOLUTION for this exact slot shows room-vernacular, not room-harmonic, as the pack that actually 'governs' by precedence, even though the inherited prose rule described a harmonic-mean derivation the resolved numbers do not use. The identical bug and fix recur on mexican-colonial.kit.json and mexican-hacienda.kit.json (via a different lineage path) and, as a Palladian-flavoured variant, on arts-and-crafts-british.kit.json. Fixed here per WP-4.2 instructions rather than on any ancestor kit."
    },
